Ladybird now hands a per-site RequestServer its site over IPC, before
any client connects, and none of its processes names a site on its
command line. Chrome 158 and Safari 26.6.2 (WebKit) were each run in a
macOS 26.6.2 VM with example.com and wikipedia.org open, and ps showed
no site in the arguments of any of their helper processes. Chrome’s
single network.mojom.NetworkService utility serves every site, and its
renderers carry only flags such as --renderer-client-id. And WebKit’s
com.apple.WebKit.Networking and WebContent XPC services run with no
arguments at all. Gecko wasn’t run (a fresh profile stops at its Terms
of Use dialog), but its source does the same as this change: Its
ContentParent puts only flags on a content process’s command line, and
sends the remote type, which names the site of a Fission process, in a
SetRemoteType message once the process is up and running.
